  '051': make({ answer: 'A butt joint stays square when the parts are cut square, the assembly is held against a square reference, pilot holes prevent splitting, and clamp pressure closes the joint without sliding it. Screws reinforce alignment; they do not correct bad ends.', setup: 'Cut matching scrap square, dry-fit the corner, mark screw centers away from edges, and stage clamps, square, driver, glue, and cleanup before opening the bottle.', steps: ['Check both mating ends with a square.', 'Predrill clearance through the first part and pilot into the second.', 'Dry-clamp against a square block or corner caul.', 'Spread a thin continuous glue film where long grain can bond.', 'Bring the parts together without smearing the reference faces.', 'Tighten clamps only until the joint closes.', 'Drive screws, then compare diagonals before the glue sets.'], checks: ['The faces finish flush.', 'The corner remains at 90 degrees.', 'Screws do not split or bulge the edge.', 'The assembly diagonals agree.'], fixes: ['If the joint slides, add a temporary stop or pin rather than more clamp pressure.', 'If the corner closes at the front but gaps at the back, recut the end square.', 'If the screw spins, repair the pilot hole before loading the joint.'], practice: 'Assemble four scrap corners with different clamp orders and record which sequence holds square with the least correction.' }),
  '052': make({ answer: 'Pocket holes are smart for face frames, shop fixtures, cabinet assembly, and hidden reinforcement when the screw enters sound long grain. Avoid them where movement, exposed holes, very thin stock, end-grain withdrawal, or repeated structural load works against the screw.', setup: 'Use the jig, screw type, and bit setting specified for the actual stock thickness. Clamp the jig and the joint; a pocket screw can pull an unsecured part out of alignment.', steps: ['Mark the show and hidden faces.', 'Set jig and stop collar from measured stock thickness.', 'Drill in sound stock away from edges and defects.', 'Clamp the mating parts flush and square.', 'Drive the specified pocket screw at controlled torque.', 'Stop when the head seats; do not crush the pocket.', 'Inspect the exit risk before placing a second screw.'], checks: ['The screw does not break through a face or edge.', 'The joint stays flush while tightened.', 'The receiving grain can resist withdrawal.', 'The joint does not lock a wide solid panel across its grain.'], fixes: ['A stripped pocket needs a sound plug or relocated screw, not a larger random fastener.', 'If the parts creep, improve clamping before driving again.', 'Choose a dado, mortise-and-tenon, dowel, or mechanical connector when the load path needs geometry.'], practice: 'Build a small face frame and a right-angle box corner in scrap, then compare which load direction each pocket joint resists.' }),
  '054': make({ answer: 'Set the circular-saw blade to half the combined joint thickness, make closely spaced kerf cuts across the waste, remove the fins, and pare the floor flat. Cut both mating pieces from the same depth setting so their faces finish flush.', setup: 'Mill equal-thickness stock, mark the lap length from the actual mating part, clamp both pieces firmly, and support the saw shoe across the whole cut. Keep hands outside the blade path.', steps: ['Mark the shoulder around the stock from one reference face.', 'Set blade depth with a test cut in matching scrap.', 'Clamp a square guide at the shoulder.', 'Cut the shoulder on the waste side.', 'Make parallel relief cuts across the lap waste.', 'Break out the thin fins with controlled pressure.', 'Pare or rout the floor to the shoulder line.', 'Dry-fit and adjust only marked high spots.'], checks: ['Both outside faces finish flush.', 'Shoulders close without gaps.', 'The lap bears across its full floor.', 'The joint seats by hand.'], fixes: ['A deep floor makes a loose joint and usually requires a remade part or deliberate shim.', 'If the floor slopes, improve shoe support and flatten only the high area.', 'If shoulders gap, correct shoulder squareness before thinning the lap.'], practice: 'Cut three half-laps from one depth setting and stack the assemblies to compare face alignment.' }),
