  '056': make({ answer: 'For a hand-cut mortise and tenon, cut the mortise first, size the tenon from that opening, and preserve one reference face. Chop the mortise in small steps, saw the tenon cheeks on the waste side, then pare only the marked high spots.', setup: 'Mill straight square stock, select a mortise chisel about one-third of the member thickness, set one mortise gauge from that chisel, and mark the reference faces and joint depth.', steps: ['Gauge both mortise walls from the reference face.', 'Knife the tenon shoulders from one setting.', 'Chop the mortise short of full depth, working inward from both ends.', 'Clear waste without levering against finished walls.', 'Saw tenon cheeks just outside the gauge lines.', 'Saw shoulders without undercutting them.', 'Test the tenon and mark burnished high spots.', 'Pare in thin cuts until shoulders close.'], checks: ['Mortise walls are sound and parallel.', 'The tenon seats by hand with even resistance.', 'Shoulders close on all sides.', 'Enough wood remains around the mortise for the load.'], fixes: ['A tapered mortise needs its high wall corrected, not a tapered tenon.', 'A loose cheek can sometimes be repaired with a deliberate long-grain veneer; major error needs a new part.', 'Do not drive a tight tenon into a dry mortise because it can split the member.'], practice: 'Cut two identical joints from the same gauge setting and compare shoulder closure and face alignment.' }),
