  '060': make({ answer: 'A box-joint jig works when the indexing pin equals the cutter width and its distance from the cutter equals that same width. Cut one test pair, shift the pin or fence by half the observed error, and do not begin the project until the fingers seat by hand.', setup: 'Confirm dado-stack compatibility or use repeated passes with a suitable blade. Fit a zero-play runner, build a tall square backer, and keep every fastener outside the cutter path.', steps: ['Cut a test slot with the final cutter setup.', 'Make and fit the indexing pin to that slot.', 'Place the pin exactly one cutter width from the cutter.', 'Clamp sacrificial backers behind the work.', 'Cut the first slot against the pin.', 'Index each new slot over the pin without debris.', 'Offset the mating board by one finger.', 'Test the full joint before cutting project parts.'], checks: ['Fingers seat with firm hand pressure.', 'Outside faces finish flush.', 'The pattern ends cleanly at both edges.', 'The work remains square to the table.'], fixes: ['If the joint is uniformly tight or loose, move the pin by half the total error.', 'If error grows across the board, pin width and spacing differ.', 'If edges chip, replace backers and check cutter sharpness and feed.'], practice: 'Cut two pairs in scrap, label the pin adjustment, and keep the accepted pair beside the jig.' }),
